Third parties used or referenced by this project
================================================

1. MuJoCo (https://github.com/google-deepmind/mujoco), Apache License 2.0, Google DeepMind.
   The camera images under site/frames/ were rendered with MuJoCo 3.14.0 from simulated episodes. The simulator under
   simulator/ needs MuJoCo 3.14.0 and NumPy 2.5.3, which the user installs from PyPI (simulator/requirements.txt,
   each package under its own license). The second pilot (assembly_pilot/) ran MuJoCo 3.14.0 inside a Nebius AI Cloud
   Serverless Job (see assembly_pilot/trial/requirements.txt). MuJoCo itself is not included in or redistributed by
   this repository.

2. MuJoCo Menagerie, model "franka_emika_panda" (https://github.com/google-deepmind/mujoco_menagerie),
   Apache License 2.0. The Menagerie describes this model as derived from Franka Emika's publicly available
   franka_description. The simulated arm seen in the images under site/frames/ is this model as the robot code under
   simulator/ (siteagent/world/build.py) loads and edits it in memory (keyframes removed; a tool site and a wrist
   camera added; the gripper force raised about ten times; gravity compensation of the arm's own weight), placed in a
   kitchen scene written by the team. simulator/assets/fetch_panda.sh downloads the model from the Menagerie
   repository at commit 71f066ad0be9cd271f7ed58c030243ef157af9f4 and checks its 70 files (panda.xml, its meshes, its
   LICENSE and README) against simulator/assets/PANDA_ASSETS.sha256; the download stays on the user's machine.
   AssembleNIST (item 4) ships the same model, edits it in memory with its own scene builder, and used it in the
   second pilot. No Menagerie file (model, mesh or texture) is included in or redistributed by this repository.
   Citation: Zakka, Tassa and the MuJoCo Menagerie Contributors, "MuJoCo Menagerie: A collection of
   high-quality simulation models for MuJoCo", 2022.

3. NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Super (nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b), NVIDIA Nemotron Open Model License
   (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/agreements/enterprise-software/nvidia-nemotron-open-model-license/; the license named
   on the model card https://huggingface.co/nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Super-120B-A12B-FP8, both opened on October 27,
   2026), called at run time through Nebius Token Factory (an OpenAI-compatible inference API, used under its own
   terms of service).
   No model weights are included in or redistributed by this repository. The repository contains text that the model
   returned: the answers of two calls (recorded/, simulator/recorded_choices/ and site/data/episodes.js), the
   separate reasoning text of those two calls (simulator/recorded_choices/), and, in the earlier recorded episodes
   shown on the page, programs the model wrote. Nemotron is not used in the second pilot.

4. AssembleNIST (https://github.com/suniique/assembly-nist), MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Tao Sun.
   Referenced only. The second pilot fetched it at commit eb5d15e004591d380c0d521da67e025ebee37703 and ran its
   pretrained checkpoint e8192_it12500.pt, unchanged, inside a disposable Nebius AI Cloud Serverless Job. No
   AssembleNIST code, checkpoint, asset, render, image or video is included in or redistributed by this repository:
   assembly_pilot/ holds only our own trial programs, the JSON they exported from the Job, our recount, and a chart
   and a slide drawn from that recount. AssembleNIST is one policy for the 20 assemblies of NIST Assembly Task
   Board 1; the second pilot evaluated it on its task R16 only. AssembleNIST describes itself as an independent
   implementation that builds on ideas from the AlphaNIST paper (Zhang et al., "AlphaNIST: Emergent Assembly Skills
   from Binary Task Rewards", arXiv:2610.13354, https://alpha-nist.github.io/) and the SGS paper (Zhang et al.,
   "A Balanced Data Diet: Addressing the Exploration Bottleneck in Mega-Scale RL for Robot Control",
   arXiv:2610.12465, https://sgs-rl.github.io/), with no code from those projects and no affiliation with or
   endorsement by their authors. It is not NVIDIA code. AssembleNIST asks to be cited together with AlphaNIST and
   SGS; the three references are in README.md and assembly_pilot/README.md.

5. NIST Assembly Task Board 1 part and fixture meshes and board layout, used by AssembleNIST, come from UW Lab
   (https://github.com/UW-Lab/UWLab); AssembleNIST marks their redistribution license "to be confirmed before
   redistribution". None of them, and no image of them, is included in this repository. The NIST assembly task boards
   are described by NIST at
   https://www.nist.gov/el/intelligent-systems-division-73500/robotic-grasping-and-manipulation-assembly/assembly

6. Python packages installed inside the Job only, not included in this repository, each under its own license:
   PyTorch 2.14.1 (CPU build) and the packages pinned in assembly_pilot/trial/requirements.txt (among them NumPy,
   SciPy, imageio and Pillow).

7. Nebius Token Factory and Nebius AI Cloud (Serverless Jobs) are services, used under their own terms.

Fonts and libraries
===================

No font file and no third-party library is included. The page under site/ is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no
library and no external resource; it uses the viewer's system fonts. The client, the tools, the tests and
assembly_pilot/recount.py use the Python standard library only; the simulator needs MuJoCo and NumPy (item 1).
assembly_pilot/chart.svg names system fonts and embeds none; assembly_pilot/slide_1280x720.png is a picture drawn with
system fonts and embeds no font file.

NVIDIA, Nemotron, Nebius, Token Factory, NIST, MuJoCo and Franka Emika are named descriptively only; this project is
not endorsed by them.

The web addresses in this file come from the upstream projects' own files or from our notes. Each one was opened on
October 27, 2026, except the UW Lab address, which comes from AssembleNIST's files and was not opened.
